<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Micaela Bastidas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1744">1744</a>–<a href="/years/1781">1781</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Micaela Bastidas Puyucahua</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Micaela Bastidas Puyucahua was an Indigenous leader who, with her husband <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tupac-amaru-ii">Túpac Amaru II</a>, led a rebellion against Spanish rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, and is regarded as a martyr for Peruvian independence. Born in Pampamarca in the province of Canas in 1744, she married José Gabriel Condorcanqui in 1760 and was a full partner in his enterprises. When the rebellion began in November 1780 she supplied the troops with money, food, clothing and weapons, became acting chief after the victory at Sangarará, and urged a swift march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>. Captured when the revolt failed, she was executed in the Plaza de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arma-people">Armas</a> of Cuzco in May 1781, after her son Hipólito.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/micaela-bastidas.jpg" alt="Micaela Bastidas" width="330" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Post of Peru · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stamp_of_Peru_-_Micaela_Bastidas_(cropped).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Micaela Bastidas was born on 23 June 1744, the natural daughter of Josefa Puyucahua Sisa and Manuel Bastidas, who died in 1746. Virtually nothing is known of her father, who was perhaps a priest or of African descent; some documents call her a zamba, the colonial term for a person of mixed African and indigenous ancestry, while her marriage certificate lists both parents as Spaniards, a label that may have been a sign of respectability. As an illegitimate child she was marginalised in the overwhelmingly indigenous highlands. She spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> better than Spanish, was a devout Catholic and had little formal schooling.</p><p>She married on 25 May 1760, before her sixteenth birthday, in the church of Our Lady of Purification in Surimana. Her husband, a descendant of the Inca Túpac Amaru I, was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">cacique</a> of Pampamarca, Tungasuca and Surimana in 1764 and traded across a wide network with 350 mules. They lived in Tinta and had three sons, Hipólito, Mariano and Fernando. Letters exchanged early in the rebellion show endearments and concern for each other's safety.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The rebellion opened on 4 November 1780, when the local Spanish commander Antonio de Arriaga was taken prisoner; he was later hanged. The rebels made their headquarters at Tungasuca and defeated the Spanish at Sangarará on 18 November. Because indigenous people were forbidden firearms, obtaining weapons was among the hardest of her tasks. She also issued safe-conducts, took charge of the rearguard, guarded against espionage and organised a service of mounted chasquis that carried messages across rebel territory.</p><p>Local rulers called her Señora Gobernadora, La Coya or La Reina, and one description calls her a superior strategist to her husband and certainly more daring. He held off from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cuzco</a>, which allowed the Spaniards to bring in reinforcements. By March 1781 the rebel army numbered seven thousand men and women.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 18 May 1781 the prisoners were taken to be executed. Hipólito had his tongue cut out and was hanged. Micaela Bastidas fought her executioners until they subdued her and cut off her tongue. A hand-cranked garrote made for the occasion could not be used because her neck was so slender, and she was strangled with a rope, beaten with a club and kicked to death, in front of her husband, who was then killed. Their bodies were dismembered and the parts sent to towns of the region for display. The involvement of women in such risings shocked the Spanish authorities, who gave them the same penalties as men.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Micaela Bastidas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The priestess Cécile Fatiman presided with Boukman over the ceremony at Bois Caïman in August 1791; <b>Micaela Bastidas</b> commanded rebels beside her husband Túpac Amaru II and was executed in Cusco in 1781; and in 1900 Yaa Asantewaa led the last armed resistance to British rule in Asante.</blockquote><a href="/essays/from-ruler-to-enslaved">From Ruler to Enslaved</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Micaela Bastidas</b>, of Indigenous and African descent, commanded rebels beside her husband Túpac Amaru II and was executed in Cusco in 1781.</blockquote><a href="/societies/spanish-america">Social Hierarchy of Spanish America</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tamburco District, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. Died in Plaza de Armas, in what is now <a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-13.62,-72.87&amp;year=1768" aria-label="Tamburco District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2553 2671 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Micaela Bastidas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3003 2941h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3003 2941h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-13.62,-72.87&amp;year=1768">What was here in 1768? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
